drm/panel: add the Samsung AMS678UW01 panel (OnePlus 8 Pro)

The 6.78" 1440x3168 DSC panel of the OnePlus 8 Pro (instantnoodlep),
sold as Samsung AMS678UW01 and called ANA6706 in Qualcomm's vendor
trees. Same panel, two names.

The driver is darknagel's, from oneplus-instantnoodlep-nura-unofficial
(kernel-patches/0002 and 0003), where it lights this panel on mainline
at 1440x3168@120 and at 1080x2376@60/120. It replaces a hand-written
driver of ours that got every software-side indicator green -- panel
out of sleep, err_count 0, vsync counting, underrun 0 -- and a black
screen. The point of taking it is not that it is better written; it is
that it is known to work on this hardware, so a remaining black screen
is then a kernel or DPU problem and not a register sequence problem.

What it does that is worth knowing before editing it:

  - the panel runs in video burst mode (MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO_BURST |
    NO_EOT_PACKET | CLOCK_NON_CONTINUOUS), with MODE_LPM set only
    around DCS accesses rather than permanently;
  - the DSC PPS goes out twice, as the vendor-private DCS 0x9e write
    in the init sequence and again as a standard DTYPE_PPS packet in
    prepare(); the DPU encodes from the same drm_dsc_config, so the
    two sides agree by construction;
  - only DSC geometry is configured (slice size, slice count, bpc,
    bpp, block prediction); the RC parameters are whatever the shared
    helpers derive from it;
  - the four vendor timings differ in DSC geometry, so one is chosen
    at probe time by the "mode" module parameter rather than switched
    at runtime.

Differences from the upstream version, and why:

  - supplies: this board's device tree lists four panel rails (vdd,
    vddio, vci, and px-v18r for the Pixelworks Iris bridge) where the
    upstream port lists two. All four are named here, because these
    rails have no other consumer and an unreferenced regulator is
    switched off at late init.

Nothing else is changed. Every API it uses -- devm_drm_panel_alloc(),
the _multi DCS helpers, devm_regulator_bulk_get_const() and
<linux/device-id/of.h> -- exists in 7.2.8, checked against the
tarball rather than assumed.

The one thing it does not do is touch the Iris coprocessor, and it
does not have to: reading gpio112 on this phone returns 1, meaning the
bootloader already left Iris in analog bypass.

Generated by scripts/regen-op8p-panel-patch.sh from
drivers/panel-samsung-ams678uw01.c and bindings/samsung,ams678uw01.yaml;
the Kconfig and Makefile hunks are diffed against 7.2.8.
